course: world
lesson: 89
title: Saving water and energy at home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Almost all the water on Earth is salty sea water; only a tiny part is fresh water we can drink, and most of that is frozen in ice or hidden deep underground.
explain: The water from your tap has been collected, cleaned at a treatment works and pumped through pipes to your home, and after you use it, it must be cleaned again — every step needs energy.
explain: Hot water costs twice: once for the water and again for the energy that heated it, so a short shower instead of a full bath saves both.
explain: In a home most energy is used for heating, cooling and hot water; keeping heat in with thick walls, roof insulation and closed windows saves more than anything else.
explain: Example: a tap that drips once a second wastes thousands of litres a year — enough to fill dozens of baths — just because a small washer needs changing.
fact: About 97 out of every 100 litres of water on Earth are salty sea water.
fact: Most of the Earth's fresh water is frozen in ice caps and glaciers or lies deep underground.
fact: Tap water is cleaned at a water treatment works and used water is cleaned again at a sewage works.
fact: Pumping and cleaning water uses energy, so saving water also saves energy.
fact: Heating water uses a lot of energy, so saving hot water saves the most.
fact: A short shower usually uses much less water than a full bath.
fact: A dripping tap can waste thousands of litres of water in a year.
fact: Insulation in the roof and walls keeps heat inside a house in winter, and closed curtains on winter evenings help too.
fact: An LED bulb gives the same light as an old filament bulb with far less electricity.
fact: Many devices still use electricity on standby when they look switched off.
fact: Growing food and making clothes also uses a great deal of water, so wasting food wastes water too.
exercise: Why can we drink only a small part of the Earth's water? | Most of it is salty or frozen | Most of it is too warm to drink | Most of it is in the clouds | Most of it is used by plants
exercise: Why does saving tap water also save energy? | Water must be pumped and cleaned using energy | Water makes electricity as it flows out of taps | Tap water is heated before it reaches every tap | Pipes need electricity to stay cold
exercise: Why is saving hot water better than saving cold water? | It saves the water and the energy that heated it | Hot water is more expensive to clean afterwards | Hot water evaporates out of the pipes faster | Hot water is taken from a different sea
exercise: Which usually uses less water? | A five-minute shower | A full bath | Two full baths | A bath with the shower on as well
exercise: What happens to water after it goes down the drain? | It is cleaned at a sewage works before going back to rivers | It flows straight back into the tap | It disappears into the ground for ever | It is burned to make electricity
exercise: What does roof insulation do? | It keeps heat from escaping in winter | It lets much more sunlight into the rooms | It stops rain from making noise | It makes the house lighter
exercise: Why is an LED bulb better than an old filament bulb? | It gives the same light using much less electricity | It gives brighter light by using more electricity | It works without any electricity at all | It makes the room warmer for free
exercise: What does a TV on standby do? | It keeps using a little electricity | It uses no electricity at all while it waits | It charges itself for later | It makes electricity for the house
exercise: In most homes in cold countries, what uses the most energy? | Heating the rooms and the water | Charging everybody's mobile phones | Running the doorbell all day long | Lighting one small lamp in the hall
exercise: How can a dripping tap be fixed? | By changing a worn washer inside it | By painting the tap with special paint | By turning on the hot water tap instead | By opening a window to let the drips dry
exercise: Why does throwing food away also waste water? | Lots of water was used to grow it | Food is made mostly of tap water | Food must be washed before it goes in the bin | Bins are cleaned with fresh water
exercise: Which saves water when brushing your teeth? | Turning the tap off while brushing | Leaving the tap running gently the whole time | Brushing with hot water from the tap | Brushing twice as long as you usually do
exercise: Where is most of the Earth's fresh water? | In ice and deep underground | In rivers, streams and big lakes | In the clouds high up in the sky | In water tanks and plastic bottles
exercise: Why do people close curtains on winter evenings? | They help keep heat inside the room | They make the heater work harder | They let cold air in more slowly at noon | They stop the lights from using electricity
test: A family takes baths every day and then switches to short showers. What two things do they save? | Water and the energy to heat it | Only the soap they use | Only the cold water | Nothing, because showers use more water
test: Which is true? | Saving water helps save energy as well | Tap water comes to us without using any energy | Most water on Earth is fresh and drinkable | A dripping tap wastes only a cupful a year
test: Two identical houses are heated equally. One has a thick insulated roof. What happens on a cold night? | The insulated house stays warmer for longer | Both houses lose heat at exactly the same rate | The insulated house gets colder faster | The house without insulation stays warmer
test: Tom leaves his game console on standby all year. What happens? | It uses electricity all year even when not played | It uses no electricity because the screen is dark | It stores extra energy for later games | It makes the console last for ever
test: A country has a long dry summer. Why is wasting water a bigger problem then? | Less fresh water is left in rivers and storage lakes | The sea becomes fresh in summer | Pipes freeze and burst in the heat | Plants stop needing water in summer
test: Which change in a school would save the most energy in a cold country? | Insulating the walls and roof | Using pencils instead of pens | Turning off one lamp for a minute | Opening the windows in winter
test: Mia says, "Water is free because it falls from the sky." What is she missing? | Cleaning and pumping it to taps costs energy and money | Rain is too salty to fall on cities | Rain water never reaches rivers | Taps make the water themselves
test: Why can wasting a meal also waste water? | Growing the food needed a lot of water | Food turns into sea water once it is in the bin | Cooking food creates new water | Plates must be washed in the river
test: A school replaces 100 old bulbs with LED bulbs. What happens? | The rooms stay as bright but less electricity is used | The rooms get darker and less electricity is used | The rooms stay as bright but more electricity is used | The bulbs stop needing electricity
test: shower or bath, which saves more water | A short shower usually uses much less water | A full bath usually uses much less water than a shower | They always use exactly the same water | A long shower uses less than any bath
test: telly's off but the little light's still on, is it using power | Yes, many devices use electricity on standby | No, it uses no power at all when the screen is off | No, the light makes its own power | Yes, but only at night
test: Ava says saving water has nothing to do with saving energy. true? | No, pumping, cleaning and heating water all use energy | Yes, water and energy are not linked at all | Yes, water comes to homes with no energy | No, saving energy wastes water instead
test: my cousin reckons a dripping tap only wastes a few drops. right? | No, a dripping tap can waste thousands of litres in a year | Yes, the drops are too small to matter | Yes, it wastes less than a cup a year | No, a dripping tap saves water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the reason for which saving hot water saves the most energy? | Heating water uses a great deal of energy | Hot water is more costly to pump than cold | Hot water evaporates more slowly than cold | Cold water requires no cleaning
exam: Identify the bulb which gives equal light with far less electricity. | An LED bulb | An old filament bulb | A bulb of greater power | A coloured glass bulb
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning devices left on standby. | Many still use electricity when they look switched off | They use no electricity whatsoever | They generate electricity for the home | They use electricity only during the day
exam: Ascertain the function of insulation in the roof and walls of a house. | It keeps heat inside the house in winter | It draws heat into the house from the ground | It increases the loss of heat in winter | It cools the house by admitting wind
